\details{
For \code{aov} models (including repeated-measures models with an \code{Error()} term) and \code{afex} models, \code{apa_parameters()} returns the ANOVA table from \code{\link[=apa_anova]{apa_anova()}}. \code{predictor_parameters} and \code{t_with_df} are ignored, and other arguments in \code{...} (e.g., \code{effect_size}, \code{type}) are passed to \code{\link[=apa_anova]{apa_anova()}}. To display regression coefficients instead, refit the model with \code{\link[stats:lm]{stats::lm()}}.

For structural equation models and confirmatory factor analyses fit with \code{lavaan}, estimates come from \code{\link[lavaan:standardizedSolution]{lavaan::standardizedSolution()}} (\code{standardize = TRUE}, default) or \code{\link[lavaan:parameterEstimates]{lavaan::parameterEstimates()}} (\code{standardize = FALSE}). Each kind of parameter is introduced by a heading row: "Factor loadings" (e.g., Visual \eqn{\rightarrow}{->} x1), "Regressions" (e.g., Speed \eqn{\leftarrow}{<-} Visual), "Correlations" (or "Covariances", if unstandardized; e.g., Visual \eqn{\leftrightarrow}{<->} Textual), and "Defined parameters" (e.g., indirect effects defined with \verb{:=}), followed, if requested, by variances, intercepts, and thresholds. The default columns are the estimate, its standard error, and its \emph{p}-value; \code{"z"}, \code{"CI"}, and (with \code{standardize = TRUE}) \code{"Coefficient"} for unstandardized estimates can be added to \code{predictor_parameters}. Unstandardized parameters fixed to set the scale of a factor (e.g., marker loadings fixed at 1) are not tested. Variable names are shown as written in the model, with the first letter capitalized and underscores replaced by spaces.

For multiple-group models (e.g., \code{group = "school"}), the groups are shown side by side by default, with one row for each parameter and a column spanner for each group, so that the groups can be compared row by row. With \code{groups = "rows"}, each group has a heading row, with the kinds of parameters indented below it. A defined parameter that uses the labels of one group is shown with that group. One that combines groups (e.g., the difference between two groups' paths) is shown in the first group's columns, or under an "Across groups" heading. \code{\link[=apa_note]{apa_note()}} gives the size of each group and names the parameters constrained to be equal across groups (\code{group.equal}), and any exceptions (\code{group.partial}). Standardized estimates of equal parameters can still differ across groups, because standardization uses each group's variances.

For multinomial logistic regression models (\code{nnet::multinom}), each outcome
category except the reference category (the first level of the response)
has its own block of coefficients, introduced by a heading row such as
"Part time vs. Not working". Coefficients are log odds of the category
relative to the reference category, tested with Wald \emph{z} tests, and their
exponentiated values (shown by default) are odds ratios relative to the
reference category (sometimes called relative risk ratios), labeled \emph{OR}.
To compare the categories with a different reference, relevel the response
(e.g., with \code{\link[stats:relevel]{stats::relevel()}}) before fitting the model. \code{\link[=apa_note]{apa_note()}} names
the reference category.

For zero-inflated and hurdle count models fit with \code{pscl::zeroinfl()} and
\code{pscl::hurdle()}, the count model and the zero model are each introduced by
a heading row ("Count model" and "Zero-inflation model" or "Zero hurdle
model"). Coefficients are tested with Wald \emph{z} tests. Only the count model's
coefficients are exponentiated (by default), giving incidence rate ratios
(\emph{IRR}), because the zero model has a different link and meaning. In a
zero-inflated model, the zero model predicts excess zeros (zeros not
produced by the count model); in a hurdle model, it predicts whether the
count is greater than zero. \code{\link[=apa_note]{apa_note()}} says which, names the count
distribution, and gives the negative binomial dispersion parameter
(\eqn{\theta}{theta}).

For \code{glm} objects (including \code{MASS::glm.nb}), the test statistic is \emph{z} for families with a fixed dispersion parameter (binomial, Poisson, negative binomial) and \emph{t} for families with an estimated dispersion parameter (Gaussian, Gamma, inverse Gaussian, quasi families). Whichever statistic applies is displayed. The exponentiated coefficient column (\code{Exp_Coefficient}) is labeled \emph{OR} for logit links, \emph{IRR} for log links in count models (Poisson, quasi-Poisson, negative binomial), \emph{RR} for log links in binomial models, and Exp(\emph{B}) otherwise.

For ordinal regression models (\code{MASS::polr} and \code{ordinal::clm}), coefficients are tested with Wald \emph{z} tests. (\code{parameters} reports \emph{t} for \code{polr}, but the test is asymptotically normal, and \code{clm} reports \emph{z} for the same model.) With a logit link, exponentiated coefficients are cumulative odds ratios, labeled \emph{OR}. Threshold rows show only the estimate, its standard error, and its confidence interval, because their exponentiated values are not odds ratios, and testing whether a threshold equals 0 is not meaningful. Confidence intervals (\code{"CI"}, \code{"Exp_CI"}) are Wald \emph{z} intervals, consistent with the \emph{z} tests.

For mixed models fit with \code{lme4} (\code{lmer} and \code{glmer}), fixed effects are displayed as for \code{lm} and \code{glm} models. For linear mixed models, \emph{t} tests and confidence intervals use Kenward-Roger degrees of freedom by default (\code{ci_method = "kenward"}, which requires the pbkrtest package); pass another \code{ci_method} (e.g., \code{"satterthwaite"}, which requires lmerTest, or \code{"wald"}) to change this. When the degrees of freedom differ across fixed effects, they are shown in a \emph{df} column rather than in the \emph{t} column header. Random-effect rows show only the estimated standard deviation (\emph{SD}) or correlation (\emph{r}), or with \code{random_scale = "variance"}, the variance (\emph{Var}) or covariance (\emph{Cov}), grouped by cluster (grouping factor) under a subheading for each cluster, with nested clusters named, e.g., "Classroom in School". The fixed and random effects are introduced by heading rows (\code{effect_headings = TRUE}). The random-effects heading names the statistic of the \emph{SD} or variance rows (e.g., "Random effects (\emph{SD})"), and correlation or covariance rows name their own (e.g., "\emph{r} (Constant, Days)"). \code{\link[=apa_note]{apa_note()}} defines the symbols.

For mixed models fit with \code{glmmTMB::glmmTMB()}, fixed effects are tested with \emph{z} tests, and random effects are displayed as for \code{lme4} models. Coefficients of log and logit links are exponentiated by default (e.g., \emph{IRR} for Poisson and negative binomial models). When the model has a zero-inflation part (\code{ziformula}) or a dispersion part (\code{dispformula}), each part is introduced by a heading row ("Conditional model", "Zero-inflation model", or "Dispersion model"), and only the conditional model's coefficients are exponentiated. A dispersion parameter that is not modeled (i.e., without a \code{dispformula}) is not displayed.

For linear mixed models fit with \code{nlme::lme()}, fixed effects are tested with \emph{t} tests whose degrees of freedom are computed by nlme from the grouping levels at which each effect varies, and random effects are displayed as for \code{lme4} models. Parameters of residual correlation structures (\code{correlation}, e.g., \code{nlme::corAR1()}) and variance functions (\code{weights}, e.g., \code{nlme::varIdent()}) are displayed below the residual \emph{SD}, and \code{\link[=apa_note]{apa_note()}} names the structures.
}
